    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green. Here's what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nConsolidated the three separate duplicate checks (telephone, email, household) into a single derived `identityKey` = `normalizedTelephone + '|' + (email or empty) + '|' + householdId`. A create request is now rejected with 409 only when the whole key exactly matches an existing owner's.\n\n### New code\n- **`IdentityKey`** (helper, in `rest/function/owner`) \u2014 the one place the key is derived. `of(telephone, email, householdId)` normalizes each part by reusing the existing `TelephoneNormalizer` (E.164) and `EmailNormalizer` (lower-case, empty when absent); `forOwner(Owner)` reads the parts off an owner. Single source of truth for both the check and the response.\n- **`EnsureUniqueIdentity`** (pipeline step) \u2014 computes the built owner's key and rejects an exact full-key match against existing owners via a checked `DuplicateIdentityException` (409). It runs **after `AssignHousehold`**, because the household part of the key is the assigned household id, so the built owner carries its final key parts before the check.\n- **`DuplicateIdentityException`** + handler + `officefloor/escalation/\u2026DuplicateIdentityException.yml` (409, mirroring the existing handlers).\n\n### Behaviour change realized\nBecause telephone is part of the key, two members of the same household (same `householdId`) with different telephones now have different keys and are both allowed \u2014 only an exact full-key match is a duplicate. The acceptance test (`coreRejectsIdentityCollision`) confirms two lone owners (both with empty email and empty household id) sharing a telephone collide.\n\n### Wiring & response\n- `owners.POST.yml`: removed `ensureUniqueTelephone` / `ensureUniqueHousehold` / `ensureUniqueEmail`; inserted `ensureUniqueIdentity` after `assignHousehold` (governed by the transaction). Normalization/format-validation steps stayed.\n- `openapi.yml`: added the read-only `identityKey` field to the `Owner` schema, and `OwnerMapper` derives it (like the other computed fields) so every owner response returns it.\n\n### Cleanup\nDeleted the three obsolete `EnsureUnique*` functions, their three `Duplicate*Exception`/handler pairs and escalation YAMLs, and updated the dangling javadoc references in `TelephoneNormalizer`, `EmailNormalizer`, `HouseholdNormalizer`, `AssignHousehold`, `NormalizeAddress`, and `CountHousehold`.\n\n`./mvnw test` \u2192 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the only test class), BUILD SUCCESS.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: this removes household uniqueness for owners who don't set `sharesHousehold` \u2014 previously same-lastname+address was a 409 regardless of phone; now, since lone owners keep a null household id, two such owners with different phones both succeed. That's the intended consequence of the spec (\"only an exact full-key match is a duplicate\"), but it does mean `sharesHousehold` no longer gates anything at the check (it still controls whether the shared household id is stamped).",
